  "markdown": "## ORGANIZATIONS  ARE LOOKING   FOR NEW WAYS   TO WORK\n\n![image](https://microsoft-offers.avispl.com/_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Favisplstorage.blob.core.windows.net%2Favispl%2FPhoto.png&w=1200&q=75)\n\nThe concept of the workplace continues to evolve, this time, driven by two forces:\n\nThe demand for increased flexibility, andThe rise of AI.\n\nThese two forces are reshaping where and how work is done. This evolution of work is even more significant this time around, with everything else happening around people, places, and organizations.\n\n## People feel they are unable to keep up\n\nToday, the volume of work is accelerating faster than employees' ability to keep up.\n\nEmail overload persists and after-hours meetings are still holding steady. According to Microsoft, users spend 60% of their time on emails, chats, and meetings, which means the workday still skews toward communication. At the same time, leaders are saying that the shift to hybrid work has made it challenging to have confidence that employees are being productive.\n\nThis means, at all times, we are all expected to do even more than before, in less time and using less resources.\n\n## Do you feel people in your organizations are experiencing burnout?\n\nYesI am observing increased cases of job dissatisfaction, anxiety, and depression\n\nNoI feel employees feel motivated to come to work and have a healthy work-life balance\n\n## Hybrid has become mainstream\n\nIt's largely become clear. Employers and employees both want the best of both worlds i.e. in-person and remote. Over 70 percent of workers want flexible remote work options to continue, while over 65 percent are craving more in-person time with their teams.\n\nThe question is no longer even 'why and how to do hybrid work', but is now 'how to maximize its value'.\n\n## What is true about hybrid work for your organization?\n\n01We have a 5-day work from office policy.\n\n02Our employees are all remote and distributed across geographies\n\n03We have some days each week compulsory for people to come to the office\n\n## The return to the office has been a struggle\n\nWhile employers say getting people back to the office in person is a concern, the fact is people now expect flexibility and autonomy around how, when, and where they work.\n\nPolicy alone cannot reverse this reality. Research shows that people come in more for social connection. In fact, 78% of business decision makers and 73% employees say they need a better reason to go in than just company expectations. Rebuilding the social connection can be that strong lever to bring people back to the office.\n\n## Which statement do you agree with more about your organization?\n\nYesPeople feel less socially connected with each other and are merely focused on getting the job done\n\nNoThere is a strong bond between people and also between the leadership and the rest of the employees\n\n## Security has become more complex and dangerous\n\nAs organizations have been evolving their ways of working, 68% have experienced one or more endpoint attacks.\n\nWhile people are ready to work from anywhere, their organizations’ security may not be. In multiple cases, even a single unmanaged device has been putting the entire organization at risk. That's not all. As the number of unmanaged endpoints have increased, almost 70% reported feeling overwhelmed trying to manage hybrid work. [The State of Cybersecurity: 2025 Trends Report](https://arcticwolf.com/resource/aw-uk/arctic-wolf-2025-trends-report?lb-mode=overlay)\n\n## Rate your current security foundation and employees' readiness to ensure a secure workplace\n\nVery SecureOur systems are built on a robust security foundation that enables advanced security and compliance by default.\n\nSomewhat SecureWe have strong security systems but aren’t confident they can support any human errors or the additional requirements from AI tools\n\nNot SecureWe need more secure systems and need to drive better readiness among employees\n\n[Smart workplace with Teams](https://microsoft-offers.avispl.com/smart-workplace-with-teams)",
